<div class="border-neutral/15 bg-base-100 border-t">
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-12 sm:py-16">
		<header class="max-w-2xl">
			<p class="cubby-accent text-xs tracking-wide">Home</p>
			<h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Getting Home right</h2>
			<div class="text-base-content/85 mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
				<p>
					Home kept getting harder because Cubby kept getting more useful. I wanted the things I did every day within
					roughly one scroll and one tap, even if that made the information architecture less neat. But every useful
					thing I kept on Home made the next one harder to fit.
				</p>
				<p>
					This wasn't a neat redesign from A to B. I kept changing my mind as I used it, usually because a compromise
					that looked reasonable in isolation became annoying once the whole dashboard had to live with it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</header>

		<dl class="mt-10 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
			<div class="border-neutral/15 border-t pt-4">
				<dt class="font-semibold">One stop or progressive disclosure?</dt>
				<dd class="text-base-content/70 mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
					A clean summary is less useful if I have to leave it every time I log a task or add to a Limit. Keeping
					common actions on Home is faster, but more things start competing for attention.
				</dd>
			</div>
			<div class="border-neutral/15 border-t pt-4">
				<dt class="font-semibold">One responsive Home or two compositions?</dt>
				<dd class="text-base-content/70 mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
					For a long time mobile and desktop showed the same things in different arrangements. Eventually I was using
					the phone for now, next and quick logging, while desktop was better for overview and browsing.
				</dd>
			</div>
			<div class="border-neutral/15 border-t pt-4">
				<dt class="font-semibold">Personality or cleanliness?</dt>
				<dd class="text-base-content/70 mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
					Soft colors, illustrations and squircles made Cubby feel warmer than an admin panel. Enough of them
					together made the page noisier and gave too many things the same visual weight.
				</dd>
			</div>
		</dl>

		<div class="mt-14 max-w-2xl">
			<h3 class="text-2xl font-bold lg:text-3xl">Four answers to the same problem</h3>
			<p class="text-base-content/75 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
				The versions below weren't a march towards an obvious final design. Each one leaned harder into a different
				part of those trade-offs, then exposed a different problem.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-8 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
			<article>
				<!-- Screenshot TODO: 1440x900 desktop, zachczx/cubby@2502b125f74bc1a3bdfc791661bd17ac2526ec3c -->
				<div
					class="border-neutral/25 bg-base-200 text-base-content/45 grid aspect-[16/10] place-items-center border border-dashed text-sm">
					Desktop screenshot to add
				</div>
				<p class="cubby-accent mt-4 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide">01 · One long page + launcher</p>
				<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold">Put the everyday stuff in front of me</h4>
				<p class="text-base-content/75 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
					The early dashboard was a fairly conventional vertical page. Tasks, Limits and the other useful bits sat in
					one long flow, with the wider app launcher at the end. The argument was simple: I shouldn't need to open a
					feature page for something I did several times a day.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article>
				<!-- Screenshot TODO: 1440x900 desktop, zachczx/cubby@bf3462911a2bc54d52efb097a65a48e123644e28 -->
				<div
					class="border-neutral/25 bg-base-200 text-base-content/45 grid aspect-[16/10] place-items-center border border-dashed text-sm">
					Desktop screenshot to add
				</div>
				<p class="cubby-accent mt-4 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide">02 · Colored chapters</p>
				<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold">Make the density easier to scan</h4>
				<p class="text-base-content/75 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
					As Home grew, I grouped it into Manage, Health, Routines and Family &amp; Fun. The large soft color fields
					gave a long page obvious landmarks and made Cubby feel more like itself. They also made each group feel like
					another container around the cards inside it.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article>
				<!-- Screenshot TODO: 1440x900 desktop, zachczx/cubby@ab713495f094224d675ae04b46fb13beb1914666 -->
				<div
					class="border-neutral/25 bg-base-200 text-base-content/45 grid aspect-[16/10] place-items-center border border-dashed text-sm">
					Desktop screenshot to add
				</div>
				<p class="cubby-accent mt-4 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide">03 · Flat again</p>
				<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold">The containers became part of the noise</h4>
				<p class="text-base-content/75 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
					I stripped the category backgrounds, borders, radii and shadows back out and let spacing, headings and
					illustrations carry more of the hierarchy. It was cleaner, but that didn't settle the question either:
					without enough grouping, the categories could start running into each other again.
				</p>
			</article>

			<article>
				<!-- Screenshot TODO: 1440x900 desktop, current main; 2dc9a4a0b9818e86e15df1dbdebf91890bd4ffac is the first Needs You + tabs baseline -->
				<div
					class="border-neutral/25 bg-base-200 text-base-content/45 grid aspect-[16/10] place-items-center border border-dashed text-sm">
					Desktop screenshot to add
				</div>
				<p class="cubby-accent mt-4 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide">04 · Different composition</p>
				<h4 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold">Stop making one Home do both jobs</h4>
				<p class="text-base-content/75 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
					The bigger change was to stop treating mobile and desktop as the same dashboard at different widths.
					Desktop now starts with Needs You and puts the rest behind predictable category tabs. Mobile has its own
					Today, Recent and Apps composition instead of squeezing that desktop model onto a phone.
				</p>
			</article>
		</div>

		<div class="border-neutral/20 mt-14 max-w-2xl border-t pt-8">
			<h3 class="text-2xl font-bold">Where it has landed for now</h3>
			<div class="text-base-content/85 mt-4 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
				<p>
					I still don't want a perfectly tidy Home if tidy means making the common things slower. But I also don't
					need every useful feature visible at once. Needs You is a way to surface the few things that deserve
					attention, while the tabs keep everything else somewhere predictable.
				</p>
				<p>
					Splitting the compositions also gives mobile and desktop different visual budgets. I can keep some of the
					color and personality without asking every domain to shout at once. The data and actions underneath are
					still shared; what changes is what each screen puts in front of me.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>
